}

\subtree{\title{Microstimulation evokes focal somatotopic sensations}
\taxon{finding}
\image{conf-pitt-gaunt-somatotopy.jpg}
\p{\strong{Caption.} The distinct-qualia evidence: ICMS on the medial/lateral S1 arrays at ~\strong{60 µA} evokes \strong{focal, digit-specific tactile percepts} — individual electrodes map to individual fingers (thumb/D1 … little/D5) and hand regions along the somatotopic homunculus. Separable electrodes → separable, nameable percepts. This is the sharp end of "distinct qualia."}
}

\subtree{\title{Tissue safety — and the FDA burden}
\taxon{finding}
\image{conf-pitt-gaunt-tissue-safety.jpg}
\p{\strong{Caption.} Chronic-ICMS tissue-safety study (Rajan et al., \em{J. Neural Eng.} 12 (2015) 066018): in 3 NHPs, two arrays each, 4 h/day, 5 d/wk for 6 months at up to 100 µA / 300 Hz, stimulation caused \strong{no detectable additional tissue effect} beyond array presence, and no effect on fine motor behavior.}
\p{\strong{The speaker's emphasis: the FDA path is the hard part.} The talk framed \em{making bidirectional BCIs last a decade} as fundamentally a \strong{regulatory and durability} problem, not a neuroscience one — years of tissue-safety histology, chronic large-animal data, and demonstrated device longevity are the gating burden for an implant meant to stay in a human head for ten years. (Compare Motif's "4 years, $20M to IDE," [[conf-motif-dot]] — the same difficulty, from the device-company side.)}
}

\p{Why it matters for us: the invasive endpoint of the "distinct qualia" axis — the same axis our noninvasive multi-sensory stimulation direction ([[bcf-0056]]) approaches from the other side. Maps to \strong{[I] High-Bandwidth Invasive} (Blackrock/Utah array) in [[bcf-0051]].}
